<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-derivatives-recap" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>A Derivatives Refresher</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      This course is about <term>integral</term> calculus, but every technique
      in it leans on <term>differential</term> calculus you've already seen.
      Rather than assume that's all still fresh, we spend our first two class
      meetings reminding ourselves what a derivative actually is, and getting
      the rules for computing one back into your hands quickly. The goal here
      isn't to re-teach a topic from scratch &#8212; it's to reload the
      <em>picture</em> in your head, so the mechanics you already learned once
      make sense again instead of feeling like memorized symbol-pushing.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-derivatives-refresher">
    <title>Derivative Rules and Critical Points</title>
    <!-- Sessions 1-2 . Tue Aug 25 & Thu Aug 27 -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-what-is-a-derivative">
      <title>What does a derivative actually mean?</title>

      <p>
        Picture yourself driving. The odometer racks up total distance, and
        dividing that by elapsed time gives your <term>average speed</term>
        for the whole trip &#8212; one honest number, easy to compute. The
        speedometer is answering a stranger question: how fast are you going
        <em>right now</em>, at this exact instant? There's no stretch of time
        to divide by here &#8212; time isn't passing at an instant. And yet
        the needle gives a real, meaningful number. Figuring out what that
        number even <em>means</em>, and how to compute it for any changing
        quantity &#8212; not just a car's position, but a population, a
        temperature, an account balance &#8212; is the entire point of a
        derivative.
      </p>

      <p>
        Here's the trick that makes "speed right now" computable: you can't
        measure it directly, but you <em>can</em> measure average speed over
        a short window of time, and see what happens as that window shrinks
        toward nothing. Say your position after <m>t</m> seconds is
        <m>f(t) = t^2</m> (in some units), and you want your speed exactly at
        <m>t=1</m>. Average speed over <m>[1, 1+h]</m> is
        <m>\dfrac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h}</m>. Watch what happens as <m>h</m> shrinks:
      </p>

      <md>
        <mrow> h = 1: \qquad \dfrac{f(2)-f(1)}{1} = \dfrac{4-1}{1} = 3 </mrow>
        <mrow> h = 0.1: \qquad \dfrac{f(1.1)-f(1)}{0.1} = \dfrac{1.21-1}{0.1} = 2.1 </mrow>
        <mrow> h = 0.01: \qquad \dfrac{f(1.01)-f(1)}{0.01} = \dfrac{1.0201-1}{0.01} = 2.01 </mrow>
      </md>

      <p>
        The average speeds aren't jumping around &#8212; they're homing in on
        <m>2</m>. That limiting number, the one the average settles toward as
        the time window shrinks to a single instant, <em>is</em> the
        instantaneous speed at <m>t=1</m>. Nothing mystical is happening;
        we're just squeezing an average over a smaller and smaller window
        until, in the limit, it stops being an average of two points and
        becomes a property of one.
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="check-numeric-squeeze" label="check-numeric-squeeze">
        <title>Try it yourself</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Push the squeeze one step further than the table above. For
            <m>f(t) = t^2</m>, what is the average speed on the interval
            <m>[1, 1.001]</m>?
          </p>
          <p>
            Average speed <m>=</m> <fillin answer="2.001" mode="number"/>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
          <evaluate>
            <test correct="yes">
              <numcmp use-answer="yes" tolerance="0.001"/>
              <feedback>
                <p>
                  Right on the money &#8212; and this is even closer to
                  <m>2</m> than the <m>h=0.01</m> row was. Shrink <m>h</m>
                  further and this number keeps sliding toward <m>2</m>.
                </p>
              </feedback>
            </test>
          </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
      </exercise>

      <p>
        The same squeeze has a geometric picture, and it's the one worth
        keeping in your head all semester. Take any curve <m>y=f(x)</m> and a
        point on it. Pick a second, nearby point, and draw the line through
        both &#8212; a <term>secant line</term>. Its slope is exactly the
        average rate of change we computed above,
        <m>\dfrac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}</m>. Now let that second point slide back
        toward the first, so <m>h \to 0</m>. The secant line swings around
        and settles onto the <term>tangent line</term>: the line that just
        grazes the curve at that single point, tilted at exactly the curve's
        own steepness there. That settling slope is the derivative.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-secant-to-tangent">
        <caption>Dragging <m>Q</m> toward <m>P</m> turns the secant line into
        the tangent line.</caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-secant-tangent" label="interactive-secant-tangent"
                     platform="jsxgraph" width="100%" aspect="4:3"
                     source="jsxgraph/secant-to-tangent.js">
          <slate xml:id="interactive-secant-tangent-slate" surface="jsxboard" aspect="4:3"/>
          <instructions>
            <p>
              <m>P=(1,1)</m> is fixed on <m>y=x^2</m>. Drag the orange point
              <m>Q</m> along the curve toward <m>P</m> and watch the secant
              slope shown above the graph. What number does it approach?
            </p>
          </instructions>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <insight>
        <title>The big idea</title>
        <p>
          A derivative is nothing more than: <term>the slope an average rate
          of change settles into as the interval it's averaged over shrinks
          to a single point.</term> Everything in this subsection &#8212; the
          formula below, the rules in the next subsection, all of it &#8212;
          is just bookkeeping built on top of that one idea.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        Writing that squeeze down symbolically, instead of re-deriving it
        with a table of numbers every time, gives the formal definition:
      </p>

      <definition xml:id="def-derivative">
        <title>The derivative</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The <term>derivative</term> of <m>f</m> at <m>x</m>, written
            <m>f'(x)</m>, is
            <md>
              f'(x) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h},
            </md>
            provided the limit exists.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <p>
        You'll hear <m>f'(x)</m> described two ways, and both readings trace
        straight back to the picture above. Geometrically, it's the
        <term>slope of the tangent line</term> to <m>y=f(x)</m> at <m>x</m>
        &#8212; useful whenever you care about steepness. Physically, it's an
        <term>instantaneous rate of change</term> &#8212; useful whenever you
        care about how fast something is happening right now. Velocity is
        the derivative of position; marginal cost is the derivative of total
        cost; the rate an epidemic spreads is the derivative of case count.
        Same computation, different story attached to it depending on what
        <m>f</m> represents.
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="check-derivative-meaning" label="check-derivative-meaning">
        <title>True or false?</title>
        <statement correct="no">
          <p>
            <m>f'(x)</m> tells you the <em>average</em> rate of change of
            <m>f</m> over the interval from <m>x</m> to <m>x+1</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            False. <m>f'(x)</m> is the <em>instantaneous</em> rate of
            change at the single point <m>x</m> &#8212; there's no interval
            left to average over once the limit has been taken. The average
            rate of change over <m>[x,x+1]</m> is the different number
            <m>\dfrac{f(x+1)-f(x)}{1}</m>, and generally won't match
            <m>f'(x)</m> at all.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </exercise>

      <example xml:id="example-derivative-from-definition">
        <title>Computing a derivative directly from the definition</title>
        <statement>
          <p>Let <m>f(x) = x^2</m>. Find <m>f'(x)</m> using
          <xref ref="def-derivative"/>, and check it against the numerical
          squeeze above.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>Set up the difference quotient and simplify <em>before</em>
          taking the limit &#8212; plugging in <m>h=0</m> too early gives the
          meaningless <m>0/0</m>, since we need the algebra to cancel that
          <m>h</m> out of the denominator first.</p>
          <md>
            <mrow> f'(x) &amp;= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{(x+h)^2 - x^2}{h} </mrow>
            <mrow> &amp;= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{x^2 + 2xh + h^2 - x^2}{h} </mrow>
            <mrow> &amp;= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{2xh + h^2}{h} </mrow>
            <mrow> &amp;= \lim_{h \to 0} (2x + h) </mrow>
            <mrow> &amp;= 2x. </mrow>
          </md>
          <p>
            Now <m>h</m> can safely go to <m>0</m>, since it's no longer sitting
            in a denominator. At <m>x=1</m> this gives <m>f'(1) = 2</m> &#8212;
            exactly the number our shrinking-window table was homing in on.
            The definition isn't just abstract machinery; it's the same
            squeeze, done symbolically instead of numerically, for every
            <m>x</m> at once.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>

      <p>
        That algebra worked fine for <m>x^2</m>, but imagine repeating it for
        <m>f(x) = x^{10}</m>, or <m>f(x) = \sin(x)\cos(x)</m>. The definition
        is the <em>truth</em> of what a derivative is &#8212; you should
        always be able to fall back on it &#8212; but it's a slow way to
        compute one. The rules below exist purely so you never have to run
        that limit by hand again; every single one of them is just this same
        limit, worked out once and in general, for a whole family of
        functions at a time.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-differentiation-rules">
      <title>The differentiation toolkit</title>

      <p>
        Rather than list these as symbols to memorize, here's the intuition
        behind each one &#8212; the picture that makes the formula feel
        inevitable rather than arbitrary. A clean reference table for all of
        them sits at the end of this subsection once the ideas are back in
        place.
      </p>

      <p>
        Three of the rules are close to obvious once you see them stated. A
        <term>constant</term> function never changes, so its rate of change
        is <m>0</m>. Scaling a function by a constant factor scales its rate
        of change by that same factor &#8212; if you triple every output,
        you triple how fast it's changing too. And if two quantities change
        <em>independently</em> and you add them together (two separate
        savings accounts, each earning its own interest), the combined total
        changes at the combined rate &#8212; you just add the two rates.
      </p>

      <md>
        <mrow> \text{Constant Rule:} \qquad &amp; \frac{d}{dx}[c] = 0 </mrow>
        <mrow> \text{Constant Multiple Rule:} \qquad &amp; \frac{d}{dx}[c \, f(x)] = c \, f'(x) </mrow>
        <mrow> \text{Sum/Difference Rule:} \qquad &amp; \frac{d}{dx}[f(x) \pm g(x)] = f'(x) \pm g'(x) </mrow>
      </md>

      <p>
        The <term>Power Rule</term> takes a little more picturing. Think of
        <m>x^n</m> as <m>n</m> copies of <m>x</m> multiplied together. Nudge
        <m>x</m> up by a tiny amount, and each of those <m>n</m> copies gets
        nudged &#8212; each contributing its own small sliver to the product.
        With <m>n</m> nudges each worth roughly <m>x^{n-1}</m> stacking up,
        the total rate of change comes out to <m>n</m> times <m>x^{n-1}</m>:
      </p>

      <md>
        \text{Power Rule:} \qquad \frac{d}{dx}[x^n] = n\,x^{n-1}
      </md>

      <p>
        (That picture is easiest to see for a whole number <m>n</m>, but the
        formula itself holds for any real exponent &#8212; negative,
        fractional, whatever you throw at it.)
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="check-power-rule-negative" label="check-power-rule-negative">
        <title>Quick check: Power Rule</title>
        <statement>
          <p>Which of these is <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[x^{-2}\right]</m>?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
          <choice correct="no">
            <statement><p><m>-2x^{-1}</m></p></statement>
            <feedback><p>Careful with the exponent step: the Power Rule
            drops the exponent by <m>1</m>, so <m>-2</m> becomes
            <m>-2-1=-3</m>, not <m>-1</m>.</p></feedback>
          </choice>
          <choice correct="yes">
            <statement><p><m>-2x^{-3}</m></p></statement>
            <feedback><p>Right &#8212; bring the <m>-2</m> down as a
            coefficient, then drop the exponent by one: <m>-2</m> becomes
            <m>-3</m>.</p></feedback>
          </choice>
          <choice correct="no">
            <statement><p><m>2x^{-3}</m></p></statement>
            <feedback><p>The exponent change is right, but the sign of the
            coefficient should carry over from the original <m>-2</m>.</p></feedback>
          </choice>
          <choice correct="no">
            <statement><p><m>-2x^{-1}\cdot(-2)</m></p></statement>
            <feedback><p>That mixes up two different rules. The Power Rule
            just brings the exponent down as a coefficient and lowers it by
            <m>1</m> &#8212; nothing gets multiplied twice.</p></feedback>
          </choice>
        </choices>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="check-power-rule-more" label="check-power-rule-more">
        <title>Two more Power Rule reflexes</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use the Power Rule to differentiate, then evaluate at the given
            point.
          </p>
          <p>
            If <m>f(x)=x^5</m>, then <m>f'(2) =</m>
            <fillin answer="80" mode="number"/>
          </p>
          <p>
            If <m>g(x)=\sqrt{x}</m>, then <m>g'(4) =</m>
            <fillin answer="0.25" mode="number"/>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
          <evaluate>
            <test correct="yes">
              <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
              <feedback><p>Yes &#8212; <m>f'(x)=5x^4</m>, and
              <m>5\cdot 2^4 = 80</m>.</p></feedback>
            </test>
          </evaluate>
          <evaluate>
            <test correct="yes">
              <numcmp use-answer="yes" tolerance="0.001"/>
              <feedback><p>Yes &#8212; rewrite <m>\sqrt{x}</m> as
              <m>x^{1/2}</m> first, so <m>g'(x)=\tfrac12 x^{-1/2}</m>, and
              <m>g'(4) = \tfrac12 \cdot 4^{-1/2} = \tfrac14</m>.</p></feedback>
            </test>
          </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
      </exercise>

      <p>
        The <term>Product Rule</term> has a nice picture too. Imagine a
        rectangle whose width is <m>u(x)</m> and whose height is <m>v(x)</m>,
        so its area is <m>u(x)v(x)</m>. Nudge <m>x</m> slightly, and
        <em>both</em> sides grow a little. The extra area shows up as two
        thin strips &#8212; one along the top from the width growing (worth
        <m>u' \cdot v</m>) and one along the side from the height growing
        (worth <m>u \cdot v'</m>) &#8212; plus a tiny corner sliver so small
        it vanishes in the limit. Add the two strips and you have the whole
        rule: differentiate one factor at a time, holding the other one
        fixed, and add the results.
      </p>

      <p>
        The <term>Quotient Rule</term> isn't really a separate idea &#8212;
        it's the Product Rule wearing a disguise. Write
        <m>\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}</m> as <m>f(x)\cdot g(x)^{-1}</m> and apply the
        Product Rule (plus the Chain Rule, below, to differentiate
        <m>g(x)^{-1}</m>), and the familiar formula falls out on its own
        &#8212; which is also why the minus sign and the <m>g^2</m> in the
        denominator show up right where they do.
      </p>

      <p>
        The <term>Chain Rule</term> is usually the rustiest one, and it's
        the one we'll lean on hardest all semester &#8212; substitution, our
        main integration technique in Chapter <xref ref="ch-integrals"/>, is
        really the Chain Rule run backwards. Picture two gears meshed
        together: turn the first one, and it turns the second at a rate
        scaled by the gear ratio between them. A composed function
        <m>f(g(x))</m> works the same way &#8212; an "outer" function
        <m>f</m> wrapped around an "inner" function <m>g</m>. Nudging
        <m>x</m> nudges <m>g(x)</m> at rate <m>g'(x)</m>, and that nudge to
        <m>g(x)</m> in turn nudges <m>f(g(x))</m> at rate <m>f'</m>
        (evaluated at <m>g(x)</m>, not at <m>x</m>). The two rates multiply,
        gear-ratio style:
      </p>

      <md>
        \text{Chain Rule:} \qquad \frac{d}{dx}\big[f(g(x))\big] = f'(g(x))\cdot g'(x)
      </md>

      <p>
        Alongside these rules for <em>combining</em> functions, you'll need
        the rates of a handful of specific functions on recall. Two are
        worth a quick word rather than bare memorization. <m>e^x</m> is the
        unique function that is <em>its own</em> rate of change &#8212; at
        every instant it's already growing exactly as fast as its current
        size demands, the way an account under continuous compounding grows
        faster the bigger it gets. And differentiating a wave produces
        another wave, a quarter-cycle out of step: the slope of
        <m>\sin x</m> traces out <m>\cos x</m>, whose own slope traces out
        <m>-\sin x</m>, and so on around.
      </p>

      <fact xml:id="fact-standard-derivatives">
        <title>Standard derivatives</title>
        <statement>
          <md>
            <mrow> \frac{d}{dx}[\sin x] &amp;= \cos x
              &amp; \frac{d}{dx}[\cos x] &amp;= -\sin x </mrow>
            <mrow> \frac{d}{dx}[\tan x] &amp;= \sec^2 x
              &amp; \frac{d}{dx}[e^x] &amp;= e^x </mrow>
            <mrow> \frac{d}{dx}[\ln x] &amp;= \frac{1}{x} </mrow>
          </md>
        </statement>
      </fact>

      <example xml:id="example-product-chain">
        <title>Product Rule and Chain Rule together</title>
        <statement>
          <p>Differentiate <m>f(x) = x^2 e^{3x}</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            This is a product of two functions, <m>x^2</m> and <m>e^{3x}</m>,
            so start with the Product Rule. The second factor needs the
            Chain Rule on its own: the "outside" function is
            <m>e^{(\,\cdot\,)}</m> and the "inside" function is <m>3x</m>,
            whose derivative is <m>3</m>.
          </p>
          <md>
            <mrow> f'(x) &amp;= \frac{d}{dx}[x^2]\cdot e^{3x} + x^2 \cdot \frac{d}{dx}[e^{3x}] </mrow>
            <mrow> &amp;= 2x\,e^{3x} + x^2 \cdot 3e^{3x} </mrow>
            <mrow> &amp;= e^{3x}(2x + 3x^2) </mrow>
            <mrow> &amp;= x e^{3x}(2 + 3x). </mrow>
          </md>
        </solution>
      </example>

      <example xml:id="example-quotient">
        <title>Quotient Rule</title>
        <statement>
          <p>Differentiate <m>g(x) = \dfrac{x^2 + 1}{x - 3}</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <md>
            <mrow> g'(x) &amp;= \frac{(2x)(x-3) - (x^2+1)(1)}{(x-3)^2} </mrow>
            <mrow> &amp;= \frac{2x^2 - 6x - x^2 - 1}{(x-3)^2} </mrow>
            <mrow> &amp;= \frac{x^2 - 6x - 1}{(x-3)^2}. </mrow>
          </md>
        </solution>
      </example>

      <p>
        For quick reference once the intuition above has done its job:
      </p>

      <fact xml:id="fact-basic-rules">
        <title>Basic differentiation rules, all in one place</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            For differentiable functions <m>f</m> and <m>g</m>, and a
            constant <m>c</m>:
          </p>
          <md>
            <mrow> \text{Constant Rule:} \qquad &amp; \frac{d}{dx}[c] = 0 </mrow>
            <mrow> \text{Power Rule:} \qquad &amp; \frac{d}{dx}[x^n] = n x^{n-1} </mrow>
            <mrow> \text{Constant Multiple Rule:} \qquad &amp; \frac{d}{dx}[c \, f(x)] = c \, f'(x) </mrow>
            <mrow> \text{Sum/Difference Rule:} \qquad &amp; \frac{d}{dx}[f(x) \pm g(x)] = f'(x) \pm g'(x) </mrow>
            <mrow> \text{Product Rule:} \qquad &amp; \frac{d}{dx}[f(x)g(x)] = f'(x)g(x) + f(x)g'(x) </mrow>
            <mrow> \text{Quotient Rule:} \qquad &amp; \frac{d}{dx}\!\left[\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\right] = \frac{f'(x)g(x) - f(x)g'(x)}{[g(x)]^2} </mrow>
            <mrow> \text{Chain Rule:} \qquad &amp; \frac{d}{dx}\big[f(g(x))\big] = f'(g(x))\cdot g'(x) </mrow>
          </md>
        </statement>
      </fact>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-critical-points">
      <title>Critical points</title>

      <p>
        One more piece of vocabulary, and it follows straight from the
        tangent-line picture. Imagine hiking along the graph of <m>f</m> like
        a trail. At most points the ground under your feet is tilted &#8212;
        you're climbing or descending. But right at the top of a hill, or the
        bottom of a valley, the ground goes momentarily flat: for that one
        instant, slope zero, before it tips the other way. That flatness is
        exactly <m>f'(c) = 0</m>, and it's worth naming.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-hiking-trail">
        <caption>Drag the hiker along the trail and watch the slope underfoot
        hit <m>0</m> at the hilltop and the valley bottom.</caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-hiking-trail" label="interactive-hiking-trail"
                     platform="jsxgraph" width="100%" aspect="4:3"
                     source="jsxgraph/hiking-trail.js">
          <slate xml:id="interactive-hiking-trail-slate" surface="jsxboard" aspect="4:3"/>
          <instructions>
            <p>
              Drag the red point along the trail. The green line is the
              tangent line at your feet, and its slope is printed above the
              graph. Find both spots where that slope is <m>0</m>.
            </p>
          </instructions>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <definition xml:id="def-critical-point">
        <title>Critical point</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            A <term>critical point</term> of <m>f</m> is a value <m>c</m> in
            the domain of <m>f</m> where either <m>f'(c) = 0</m> (flat
            ground) or <m>f'(c)</m> does not exist (a sharp corner or break,
            where "the slope" isn't even well-defined).
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <p>
        Critical points are exactly the <em>candidates</em> for a hilltop or
        valley bottom &#8212; not every flat spot turns out to be one (a
        trail can flatten briefly and keep climbing, like a switchback), but
        every genuine peak or valley in the interior of the trail has to be a
        critical point first. That's the entire engine behind optimization,
        which is where we're headed next: find the flat spots, then work out
        which ones are actually the top or bottom.
      </p>

      <example xml:id="example-critical-points">
        <title>Finding critical points</title>
        <statement>
          <p>Find the critical points of <m>f(x) = x^3 - 3x</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <m>f</m> is a polynomial, so <m>f'</m> is defined everywhere;
            the only critical points come from <m>f'(x) = 0</m>.
          </p>
          <md>
            <mrow> f'(x) &amp;= 3x^2 - 3 </mrow>
            <mrow> 0 &amp;= 3x^2 - 3 </mrow>
            <mrow> 0 &amp;= 3(x^2-1) </mrow>
            <mrow> x &amp;= \pm 1. </mrow>
          </md>
          <p>
            So <m>f</m> has critical points at <m>x=-1</m> and <m>x=1</m>.
            (Sketch <m>f(x)=x^3-3x</m> if you want to see why: it has a
            local max at <m>x=-1</m> and a local min at <m>x=1</m>, exactly
            the two flat spots on that curve's trail.)
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>

      <exercise xml:id="check-critical-points" label="check-critical-points">
        <title>Try it yourself</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the critical points of <m>f(x) = x^3 - 12x</m>. Enter the
            smaller value first.
          </p>
          <p>
            Smaller critical point: <fillin answer="-2" mode="number"/>
            &#160;&#160;&#160; Larger critical point:
            <fillin answer="2" mode="number"/>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
          <evaluate>
            <test correct="yes">
              <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
              <feedback><p>Right &#8212; <m>f'(x)=3x^2-12=0</m> gives
              <m>x^2=4</m>, and the smaller root is <m>x=-2</m>.</p></feedback>
            </test>
          </evaluate>
          <evaluate>
            <test correct="yes">
              <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
              <feedback><p>Right &#8212; the other solution of
              <m>x^2=4</m> is <m>x=2</m>.</p></feedback>
            </test>
          </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
      </exercise>

      <p>
        With the mechanics of differentiation and the idea of a critical
        point back in hand, we're ready for Chapter <xref
        ref="ch-optimization-approximation"/>, where "find the critical
        points" becomes the key step in solving real optimization problems.
      </p>
    </subsection>

  </section>
</chapter>
